---
/**
 * The 200-day rule, then 2x and 3x funds and the borrowed 60/40 idea. Verdict first.
 * The four headline numbers are read from figure records; the rest of the numbers come
 * from the same research pages those records cite.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { figureValue } from "~/lib/figures";

const STATS = [
  {
    value: await figureValue("timing-rule-drawdown"),
    label: "Worst fall under the rule",
    note: "Against −83.7% for holding US stocks, 1926 to 2026, simulated",
  },
  { value: "−0.73%", label: "A year, against buying and holding", note: "The same century, after trading costs" },
  { value: await figureValue("timing-rule-losing-exits"), label: "Exits that lost money", note: "Sold, then bought back higher" },
  {
    value: `${await figureValue("timing-rule-tax-cost")}%`,
    label: "A year of tax the rule itself creates",
    note: "In a top-bracket taxable account, against the same rule in a Roth",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Market timing"
  description="The 200-day rule cut the worst fall on a century of US stocks from 84% to 43% and earned 0.73% a year less than holding, with 58 of 73 exits losing money. No, unless you know you would sell at the bottom. Plus 2x and 3x funds."
  xl
>
  <Hero
    title="Should you sell when the market drops below its 200-day average?"
    lede="No, as a way to earn more. If you know you would sell everything at the bottom of a crash, the rule buys a smoother ride and a brake on the panic, for about 0.7% a year less than holding, in a retirement account only."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="No" gloss="As a way to earn more. The smaller fall is settled; the return is too close to call." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-10" />

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2 id="the-rule">The rule</h2>
    <p>Own stocks when the market is above its average price over the last 200 trading days, and sell to cash
    when it drops below, checking once a month. It is the most popular timing rule there is, and it is popular
    because it did one thing well.</p>

    <h2 id="the-fall">What it did to the worst fall</h2>
    <p>On US stocks over the century to June 2026, with a realistic trading cost, the rule's worst fall was
    <Figure id="timing-rule-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> against −83.7% for holding. That is the one
    result in the study nobody disputes, and it is mechanical: a rule that sells after a fall begins cannot sit
    through the whole of a slow one. Nothing else the rule is sold on survives the same look.</p>

    <h2 id="the-return">What it did to the return</h2>
    <p>Over the same century the rule earned 0.73% a year less than buying and holding. Its fans compare it
    with something else: a portfolio holding a constant 73% stocks and 27% cash, which is what the rule held on
    average. Against that mix it earned <Figure id="timing-rule-gap" inline showUnit={false} />% a year more,
    inside a range running between −1.4% and +2.9%, wider than the effect. Even the direction is unsettled. And
    simply holding 73% stocks and 27% cash all the time bought a worst fall of 39% over 1985 to 2026 for free,
    with no trading and no tax.</p>
    <p>The rule's wealth relative to buying and holding peaked in June 1932 and has been below that peak in every
    one of the 1,128 months since. Backtests that use monthly-averaged prices triple its apparent edge, because
    averaging manufactures the smoothness the rule trades on. The one study with enough data, sixteen countries
    over 1870 to 2020, found +0.97% a year on prices nobody could have traded, and the United States was one of
    four countries where the rule lost.</p>

    <h2 id="how-often-wrong">How often it was wrong</h2>
    <p>Of the rule's 73 exits over 1926 to 2026, <Figure id="timing-rule-losing-exits" inline showUnit={false} />
    lost money: the market was higher when the rule bought back in than when it sold. Since 1990 the count is 21
    of 25. The worst run was twelve losing exits in a row over 1948 to 1960, and since 1990 nine in a row over
    2010 to 2020, which cost 57% of what a fully invested position made. The rule pays for its one good exit in
    2008 with decades of small losses.</p>

    <h2 id="the-tax">The tax</h2>
    <p>Every exit is a sale. In a top-bracket taxable account the rule trails holding by 2.96% a year against
    1.04% in a Roth, so the tax the rule itself creates is
    <Figure id="timing-rule-tax-cost" inline showUnit={false} />% a year, two and a half times the pre-tax gap.
    Over 36 years it pays $5.15 of tax per dollar invested where holding pays $1.53. If you use it at all, use it
    only in a retirement account.</p>
    <p>One more thing counts against it here: the rule's return moves with the trend-following fund the
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Plus trend portfolio</a> already holds, and once that fund is held the
    rule's leftover return is negative.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If you know you would sell at the bottom, hold fewer stocks instead: the <a href="/portfolios/cautious/">cautious
      portfolio</a> buys a smaller fall with no trading and no tax. If you still want the rule, run it in a
      retirement account, on plain stock funds, and expect a smoother ride and a smaller return.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="borrowed-funds">2x and 3x funds, and the borrowed 60/40</h2>
    <p>A 2x fund moves twice as far as the market each day, a 3x fund three times, using borrowed money inside
    the fund. Modelled since 1927, a 3x US stock fund fell 99.9% at its worst and spent 28 years getting back to
    its old high; a 2x fund fell 98.4% and took 24 years; the plain index fell 84%. The 200-day rule on top of
    a 3x fund adds no timing skill the data can see, and it did nothing in October 1987, because a three-week
    crash cannot be exited by a 200-day average: −67% on the 3x fund against −72% for holding it. In 2022 it
    lost 35% on 3x against 25% for the index, and in a taxable account it costs about 3.25% a year in tax,
    because it sells the whole position about three times a year.</p>
    <p>The borrowed 60/40, 55% in a 3x stock fund and 45% in a 3x long government bond fund, returned 16% a year
    since 1926 with a worst fall of 93%. It lost 52% over December 1972 to September 1981 while the index gained
    60%, and 47% in 2022: a bond bull market with borrowed stocks attached. The only defensible version of any of
    this is a small 2x holding under the rule, at most 10% to 15% of a portfolio, in a retirement account, for
    someone who has decided they want borrowed stock exposure with a slow-crash brake, and even that has
    trailed a plain constant 1.3x holding since March 2009. The default is none.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/portfolios/cautious/">The cautious portfolio</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/" variant="outline">Every other idea</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
